  "script_text": "Did one telegraph message save a train from the Halifax Explosion—or do we remember the story wrong?\n\nAt 9:05 on December 6, 1917, the explosives-laden ship Mont-Blanc blew up in Halifax Harbour. Minutes earlier, railway dispatcher Vincent Coleman had sent a warning from a station just 750 feet away.\n\nThe ship had collided with another vessel, caught fire, and drifted toward Pier 6. A sailor warned the railway office. Coleman knew a passenger train was due from Saint John. His message told the rail line to hold the train.\n\nThen the blast hit. Coleman was killed. His station was crushed.\n\nDid the train stop in time? The record disagrees. One account says it had passed the last station where it could be halted. A contemporary newspaper says a dispatcher held it at Rockingham. The train survived several miles from the blast, with broken windows and minor injuries.\n\nBut the warning mattered beyond that train. The Maritime Museum says it reached railway stations from Halifax to Truro. An hour later, a fuller call for help followed. Together, they set the railway system in motion: six relief trains arrived that day with firefighters, doctors, nurses, and supplies.\n\nSo did Coleman save the train? We may never know. The warning’s reach is not in doubt.\n",
